{"id": "8a871efd-7124-43b6-b670-d01172dd2e92", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:00:50.832756", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "d6ab6e84-9da6-47d2-b059-b3650b3a10dc", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-12cc52e1-f8dd-4c85-b3a2-db7ba81e9eef", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"python3 -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.executable)\\\" && python3 -c \\\"import numpy; print('numpy', numpy.__version__)\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import scipy; print('scipy', scipy.__version__)\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import networkx; print('networkx', networkx.__version__)\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import sklearn; print('sklearn', sklearn.__version__)\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import pandas; print('pandas', pandas.__version__)\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import pgmpy; print('pgmpy', pgmpy.__version__)\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import causalnex; print('causalnex ok')\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import castle; print('castle ok')\\\" 2>&1; python3 -c \\\"import matplotlib; print('matplotlib', matplotlib.__version__)\\\" 2>&1\", \"exit_code\": 1, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.114, \"output\": \"/usr/bin/python3\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'numpy'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'scipy'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'networkx'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'sklearn'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pandas'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pgmpy'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'causalnex'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'castle'\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'matplotlib'\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "d6ab6e84-9da6-47d2-b059-b3650b3a10dc", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 11}
{"id": "e53d811d-b8b8-4a43-b490-0a3a83ae2250", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:01:53.959430", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "8a871efd-7124-43b6-b670-d01172dd2e92", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-8a2f78df-31cb-4195-ad0f-b203ef13d652", "tool_call": {"id": "call-8a2f78df-31cb-4195-ad0f-b203ef13d652", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Propose CAMUV causal discovery experiment design\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Inputs I inspected\\n\\n**Repository state.** `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. There is no source code, no prior design notes, no reference paper, and no supplied dataset. The inline `documents.track.md` explicitly states: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"*\\n\\n**Compute / environment.** Python 3.12.3 is present at `/usr/bin/python3`. No scientific libraries are installed: `numpy`, `scipy`, `networkx`, `pandas`, `scikit-learn`, `pgmpy`, `causalnex`, `castle`, and `matplotlib` all raise `ModuleNotFoundError`. Network is disabled, GPU disabled, no datasets supplied, 24 CPU cores available.\\n\\n**Implication for the deliverable.** Since the brief is design-only and compute/repos are explicitly not mandated, the experiment design can (and should) be specification-grade on paper. Any scripts I include later (when the design is elaborated) will be **illustrative sketch only**, not proof of execution — consistent with the environment note.\\n\\n**Reference materials.** None supplied. The acronym \\\"CAMUV\\\" is not verified against any specific paper/version — treated as an assumption below.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach for the experiment design\\n\\n### 2.1 Working definition (parameterized abstract model of CAMUV, to be ratified)\\nBecause no reference was supplied, I propose to define CAMUV abstractly so the design is reproducible regardless of which specific variant the researcher has in mind:\\n\\n- **Inputs.**\\n  - Observational data matrix `D ∈ ℝ^{n×p}` over `p` observed variables.\\n  - A user-supplied candidate latent representation: e.g., initialization of `K` latent factor columns `Z ∈ ℝ^{n×K}` (e.g., PCA-style), with `K ∈ {1, 2, ..., K_max}` to be searched.\\n  - Optional: prior sparsity / edge count budget; conditional-independence test type / significance level; score-function choice.\\n- **Mechanism (schematic, order-agnostic; the researcher should confirm which of A or B matches the intended CAMUV).**\\n  - **Scheme A — latent-as-variable search.** Augment variables `X ∪ Z`, then run a constraint- or score-based causal discovery routine (e.g., PC-like CI tests, or GES on augmented variables) under the constraint that latent nodes cannot be measured. Refine `K` and `Z` by maximizing a penalized fit criterion (e.g., BIC on a linear-Gaussian SEM/score, or a kernel CI score for nonlinear).\\n  - **Scheme B — residualization / factor-model loop.** Alternately (i) fit a factor model `X = Λ Z + ε`; (ii) regress-out `ε` residual structure and apply an observed-only causal discovery routine; (iii) update `Λ` and `Z` from residual covariances and repeat until convergence.\\n- **Outputs.** (i) estimated DAG/CPG/MAG on observed variables; (ii) inferred latent set (count, per-variable latent \\\"membership\\\"); (iii) edge-type labels (direct, latent-mediated, independent).\\n\\nI will stamp every concrete choice in the elaborated design as **CAMUV-A** or **CAMUV-B** until the researcher confirms which scheme to ground on.\\n\\n### 2.2 Graphical models / data-generating processes (DGPs)\\nA factorial battery of synthetic DGPs covering the regimes the brief asks about:\\n\\n| Factor | Levels |\\n|---|---|\\n| # observed variables `p` | 5, 10, 20 |\\n| # latent confounders `K_true` | 0 (control), 1, 2 |\\n| Functional class | linear-Gaussian; nonlinear (e.g., additive models w/ post-nonlinear noise); discrete (multinomial) |\\n| Noise regime | homoscedastic Gaussian; heteroscedastic; heavy-tailed (Student-t) |\\n| Sample size `n` | 200, 1k, 10k |\\n| Graph sparsity | sparse (avg degree ≈ 1.5); dense (avg degree ≈ 3) |\\n| Latent \\\"leakage\\\" | latent parents → ≥ 3 observed children to make latent detectable |\\n\\nEach DGP is an SCM of the form `X_j = f_j(Pa(X_j) ∪ Pa_L(X_j)) + ε_j`, with `Pa_L` the set of latent parents. Ground-truth latent-to-observed edges are recorded for evaluation.\\n\\n### 2.3 Conditions / configurations (what we vary)\\n1. **CAMUV settings** (treat as hyperparameters): candidate `K_max`, CI threshold `α`, init for `Z` (random / PCA / k-means), convergence tolerance, max iterations.\\n2. **Baselines** (mandatory controls):\\n   - PC / GES on observed variables *without* latent handling → expected to mis-orient or miss latent-mediated edges.\\n   - FCI (produces a PAG, marking latent-induced dependencies).\\n   - Proxy / factor-method reference: latent-variable adaptation of a standard algorithm (e.g., LV-PCMCI, RESIT, or `gCastle`'s latent-factor methods if a concrete reference is named later).\\n   - Oracles: (a) PC given the true latents as observed; (b) random-graph and fully-connected graph — these bracket the metric floor/ceiling.\\n3. **Negative control.** Shuffle-target permutation of `D` — should drive CAMUV's latent-discovery component to near baseline, sanity-checking that gains are not artifacts.\\n\\n### 2.4 Metrics\\n- **Structural:** F1 / precision / recall on the **observational skeleton**; SHD normalized; for latent-aware methods, F1 on the projected graph (edges between observed vars, with latent-mediated edges marked).\\n- **Latent-structure recovery:** ARI / NMI between predicted and true latent-group assignments of observed variables; absolute error on `K`.\\n- **Orientation:** proportion of correctly oriented v-structures / non-colliders among recoverable triples.\\n- **Statistical calibration:** over a Monte-Carlo repetition set (≥ 100 seeds per cell), report mean + 95% bootstrap CI; check whether CAMUV's gains over baselines exceed noise envelope.\\n\\n### 2.5 Controls / feasibility\\n- **Identifiability controls:** for each DGP cell, annotate which latent configurations are identifiable in principle (e.g., overcomplete factor regimes in linear-Gaussian are non-identifiable up to rotation — we record this and either skip or fix `Z` initialization to a known subspace).\\n- **Sample-size control:** at fixed `p, K_true`, sweep `n` to locate where CAMUV's advantage over FCI emerges and where it vanishes (phase-transition style).\\n- **Runtime / resource budget:** to be set by researcher; my default proposal is ≤ 24 CPU cores, ≤ 4 h wall-clock per cell, total budget ≤ 200 single-core-hours, Monte Carlo parallelism across seeds. (Bubblewrap / no-network: design must be fully self-contained if executed.)\\n\\n### 2.6 Analysis plan (sketch)\\n1. For each cell `(p, K_true, function, noise, n, sparsity)` × `≥100` seeds, run CAMUV + baselines, record metric vectors.\\n2. Aggregate via mixed-effects model: metric ~ method + (p, K_true, function, noise, n, sparsity) + interactions; CAMUV-specific contrast vs each baseline with Holm correction.\\n3. Report effect sizes with CIs, not just p-values.\\n4. Report a **regime map** (heatmap of CAMUV-vs-best-baseline ΔF1 over `(n, K_true, function)`) as the headline figure — this is the answer to \\\"how can CAMUV discover causal relationships with unobserved common causes.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n### Assumptions (I'm *stating* these — please confirm/correct before I elaborate)\\n- **A1.** CAMUV is a latent-aware causal-discovery procedure that, given observational data and a candidate latent factor initialization, outputs an estimated causal graph on observed variables plus a latent-structure estimate. (See 2.1 for the two concrete schemes.)\\n- **A2.** The evaluation target is a fully synthetic benchmark — no real-world dataset is mandated, and the design will not assume access to one.\\n- **A3.** Latent cardinality `K_true ≤ K_max`, with `K_max ≤ 3` in the default battery (kept small because (i) identifiability degrades in overcomplete regimes, and (ii) it keeps the battery tractable).\\n- **A4.** Output graph is over observed variables only; latent structure is reported as a side-product (membership per observed variable), which is the standard convention in latent causal discovery.\\n- **A5.** Baselines are available or implementable in pure NumPy/SciPy (no preinstalled libs → if executed, a from-scratch re-implementation is required, which adds a confounder; I will flag this and refuse to claim a head-to-head against an unverified third-party implementation).\\n- **A6.** No execution is required; the deliverable is a written design only.\\n\\n### Uncertainties (focused questions I'd like answered before elaborating)\\n- **U1. CAMUV specification.** Is the intended algorithm closer to **Scheme A** (augmented-variable search) or **Scheme B** (factor-residualization loop)? A single sentence / paper reference would let me ground the design.\\n- **U2. Variable type and cardinality.** Continuous only, discrete only, or both? Practical `p` range of interest (≤ 5, ≤ 20, ≤ 100)?\\n- **U3. Latent cardinality range.** Is the interesting regime `K_true = 1–2`, or do we need to handle `K_true ≥ 3`?\\n- **U4. Functional regime.** Linear-Gaussian sufficient, or must nonlinear / discrete / mixed-type be first-class?\\n- **U5. Output type to evaluate.** DAG, MAG, PAG, or any? (Determines which metrics are primary.)\\n- **U6. Real-data component.** Should the design *include* a real-world observational dataset (e.g., as an illustrative application), or remain synthetic-only? (If real, I'd need the dataset or its description.)\\n- **U7. Budget envelope.** Any wall-clock / core-hour / disk budget if execution is later requested? Default proposal: ≤ 200 single-core-hours total.\\n- **U8. Script vs prose deliverable.** Is the elaborated design expected as a markdown document only, or also accompanied by illustrative Python sketches? I'll default to **sketches clearly labeled illustrative**, per the environment note.\\n\\n### What I will *not* assume without confirmation\\n- That CAMUV corresponds to a specific named paper, version, or reference implementation — I will not invent one.\\n- That any preinstalled library (numpy, scipy, networkx, …) is usable \\\"as-is\\\"; the absence in this environment means each is either installed first or re-implemented from scratch.\\n- That CAMUV outperforms any specific baseline — that is an empirical claim the experiment is meant to test, not a prior.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Next step.** Please confirm or amend **any** of A1–A6 and **any** of U1–U8 (especially U1 — the CAMUV scheme). On approval, I will produce the full design document at `stage=result` with rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility, and audit-quality evidence from the repository inspection above.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository inspection: `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; `find -maxdepth 3` returned no other files. Decoded task.json confirms track.md inline text \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Environment probe: `python3 --version` → 3.12.3 at `/usr/bin/python3`; explicit imports of numpy, scipy, networkx, sklearn, pandas, pgmpy, causalnex, castle, matplotlib each raised `ModuleNotFoundError`. Network disabled per public_task.environment; GPU disabled; 24 CPU cores; no datasets. No reference paper, code, or design notes supplied for CAMUV — abstract model proposed and explicitly flagged as an assumption rather than asserted as a fact.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"A6. Design-only deliverable; no executed experiment will be claimed.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"A5. No preinstalled scientific libraries — if execution were later requested, either packages must be installed or baselines + CAMUV re-implemented from scratch; the