{"id": "0b5fa8d4-7bdb-4b24-8750-9e54cc64e4c5", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:05:06.136540", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7d503cf4-1a01-415a-a215-34f0e53cfc98", "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-7d48ab18-3f43-4a61-a177-cbcd4280bc28", "tool_call": {"id": "call-7d48ab18-3f43-4a61-a177-cbcd4280bc28", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Write full design document to /workspace/experiment_design.md\",\"command\":\"cat > /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'DESIGN_EOF'\\n# Experiment Design: Discovering Causal Relationships with Unobserved Common Causes via the CAMUV Algorithm\\n\\n> **Status.** Design-only deliverable. No experiment is executed in this task.\\n> The environment note states: *\\\"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\\\"*\\n> Consistent with that note, every Python block in this document is **illustrative only** and prefixed `ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH — NOT EXECUTED`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Objective restatement & answer-shape\\n\\n**Question.** *How can you discover causal relationships in data with unobserved common causes using the CAMUV algorithm?*\\n\\n**Answer-shape.** A **regime map**: a structured empirical statement of the form\\n> *\\\"CAMUV (vs. baselines) recovers the projected observational DAG with ΔF1 ∈ [a, b] over the regime (n, K_true, functional class), and its advantage is concentrated in cells where (i) K_true ≥ 1, (ii) n is above a phase-transition threshold, and (iii) latent parents each connect to enough observed children.\\\"*\\n\\nThe regime map is the **headline figure**. It is produced by sweeping `(n, K_true, function)` at canonical `(p, noise, sparsity)`, and validated on a smaller secondary battery over the remaining factors.\\n\\n**Secondary deliverables.**\\n- Best-vs-worst baseline gap as a function of `p` and noise regime.\\n- Latent-cardinality recovery error `|K̂ − K_true|`.\\n- Negative-control null result (sanity check).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Reference definition of CAMUV\\n\\n### 2.1 Standing assumption (locked)\\nNo external reference was supplied for CAMUV. Per the researcher's locked resolution (U1), we adopt a **parameterized abstract model** and instantiate it concretely as two schemes, **CAMUV-A** and **CAMUV-B**, used consistently throughout:\\n\\n- **CAMUV-A — augmented-variable search.** Treat latent factors as additional unmeasured nodes and run a causal discovery procedure on the *augmented* set, restricted so latents are never conditioned on directly. Refine `(K, Z)` by a penalized score.\\n- **CAMUV-B — factor-residualization loop.** Alternately fit a factor model on `X`, regress residuals against observed variables for an \\\"observed-only\\\" graph, then update factors from residual covariances until convergence.\\n\\nBoth schemes consume the same inputs and produce the same outputs; they differ in how `(graph, Z, K)` are searched.\\n\\n### 2.2 Inputs (uniform)\\n| Symbol | Meaning | Default |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `D ∈ R^{n×p}` | Observational data over `p` observed variables | required |\\n| `K_max` | Maximum candidate latent count | ≤ 3 (locked, A3) |\\n| `subroutine ∈ {PC, GES}` (Scheme A); `{factor-model class, observed-only CD}` (Scheme B) | Inner discovery routine | ablated |\\n| `α` | CI test significance level | {0.01, 0.05} |\\n| `init ∈ {random, PCA, k-means}` | Factor initialization | ablated |\\n| `tol`, `max_iter` | Convergence | 1e-4, 50 |\\n\\n### 2.3 Outputs (uniform)\\n- `Ĝ`: estimated DAG on observed variables (skeleton + orientation).\\n- `K̂`: estimated latent count.\\n- `M̂`: per-observed-variable latent-membership assignment.\\n\\n### 2.4 Pseudocode\\n\\n```\\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH — NOT EXECUTED\\n# Abstract CAMUV procedure; both schemes share inputs/outputs.\\n\\ndef camuv(D, p, K_max,\\n          scheme,                 # \\\"A\\\" or \\\"B\\\"\\n          inner,                  # inner CD routine\\n          alpha=0.05,\\n          init=\\\"pca\\\",\\n          tol=1e-4, max_iter=50):\\n    n = D.shape[0]\\n\\n    # 1. Initialize latent representation Z ∈ R^{n×K}, K = 1\\n    K = 1\\n    Z = init_latents(D, K=K, method=init)\\n\\n    G_hat, score_prev = None, -np.inf\\n\\n    for it in range(max_iter):\\n        # 2a. Scheme A: search on the augmented (X ∪ Z)\\n        if scheme == \\\"A\\\":\\n            G_tilde = inner(D, Z, alpha=alpha)        # returns DAG on X∪Z\\n            G_hat   = project_observed(G_tilde, p)     # drop latent nodes\\n            Z_new   = refine_Z_from_augmented(D, Z, G_tilde)\\n\\n        # 2b. Scheme B: factor-residualization loop\\n        else:\\n            Lambda, eps = factor_model(D, Z)           # X ≈ ΛZ + ε\\n            G_hat      = inner_observed(D - eps, alpha)\\n            Z_new, K   = update_factors(D, G_hat, eps, K, K_max)\\n\\n        score = penalized_fit(D, Z_new, G_hat, K)\\n        if abs(score - score_prev) < tol:\\n            break\\n        Z, score_prev = Z_new, score\\n\\n    return G_hat, K, latent_membership(K, Z, D)\\n```\\n\\nThe above is a **specification skeleton only**. Concrete choices for `inner`, `init_latents`, `factor_model`, `refine_Z_from_augmented`, `update_factors`, `penalized_fit` are intentionally left parametric to remain faithful to the locked decision not to assert any single paper/version.\\n\\n### 2.5 Hyperparameter grid\\n\\n| Hyperparameter | Levels |\\n|---|---|\\n| `scheme` | `{A, B}` |\\n| `inner` (Scheme A) | `{PC with Fisher-z, GES with BIC-Gauss}` |\\n| `inner_observed` (Scheme B) | `{PC with Fisher-z, GES with BIC-Gauss}` |\\n| `init` | `{PCA, random normal, k-means rows of D}` |\\n| `K_max` | `{1, 2, 3}` |\\n| `α` | `{0.01, 0.05}` |\\n| `tol`, `max_iter` | fixed (see 2.2) |\\n\\nDefault operating point chosen via inner cross-validated selection on a held-out DGPs subset, then frozen before headline battery runs to avoid double-dipping.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Data-generating process (DGP) specification\\n\\n### 3.1 SCM form\\nEach DGP cell generates a DAG on `p_obs + K_true` nodes (latents included in the ground truth only, never observed), then samples from\\n\\n```\\nX_j = f_j( Pa_obs(X_j) ∪ Pa_lat(X_j) ) + ε_j ,   j = 1, …, p\\nZ_k = ε_k ,                                       k = 1, …, K_true     (# standard latent noise)\\n```\\n\\nwhere:\\n- `Pa_obs(X_j)` ⊂ observed; `Pa_lat(X_j)` ⊂ latent.\\n- Edge weights are drawn i.i.d. from `Uniform([-1.5, -0.3] ∪ [0.3, 1.5])` (avoid trivial-near-zero weights).\\n- Functional class:\\n  - **linear-Gaussian**: `f_j` linear; `ε_j ~ N(0, σ_j²)`.\\n  - **nonlinear-additive**: `f_j(X) = Σ φ_k(X_k)` with `φ_k ∈ {tanh(β·), sin(β·), polynomial degree ≤ 3}` chosen i.i.d.; `ε_j` additive.\\n- Noise regimes:\\n  - **homoscedastic Gaussian**: `σ_j ≡ 1`.\\n  - **heteroscedastic**: `σ_j ~ Uniform(0.5, 2.0)`.\\n  - **heavy-tailed**: `ε_j ~ Student-t(ν=3)` rescaled to unit variance.\\n- Sparsity:\\n  - **sparse**: expected degree per observed node ≈ 1.5.\\n  - **dense**: expected degree ≈ 3.\\n\\n### 3.2 Latent leakage rule\\nEvery latent `Z_k` has ≥ 3 observed children (otherwise the latent is empirically non-identifiable and the cell is flagged).\\n\\n### 3.3 Ground-truth recording (per cell, per seed)\\n- `G_true`: adjacency on observed nodes only (projected DAG).\\n- `O_true`: orientation matrix on observed triples.\\n- `K_true`, `per-var latent-membership matrix L_true` (latent-confounder sets).\\n- `parent_weights`, `σ_j` (for reproducibility / sanity).\\n\\n### 3.4 Out-of-scope DGP variants (explicitly listed)\\n- Discrete-only or mixed-type data (locked U2).\\n- Post-nonlinear functional class (locked U4).\\n- Time series (`n` here is i.i.d. samples, not time-indexed).\\n- Cyclic / equilibrium SCMs.\\n- Measurement error / proxy-only instruments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Factorial battery table\\n\\nThe headline regime map uses a focused 2 × 3 × 2 grid over `(n, K_true, function)` at canonical `(p=10, sparsity=sparse, noise=homoscedastic, K_max=3)`. A secondary battery varies the remaining factors at reduced seed count.\\n\\n### 4.1 Headline battery (regime map)\\n\\n| Cell | `p` | `K_true` | function | noise | sparsity | `n` | seeds |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| H1 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H2 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H3 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H4 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H5 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H6 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H7 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H8 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H9 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H10 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H11 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H12 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H13 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H14 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H15 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H16 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H17 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H18 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n\\nTotal headline runs: 18 cells × 5 algorithms × 100 seeds = **9 000 algorithm-fits**.\\n\\n### 4.2 Secondary battery (smaller sub-grid; identifies where the headline generalizes)\\n\\n| Axis varied | Levels (canonical in **bold**) |\\n|---|---|\\n| `p` | **{5, 10}, 20** |\\n| noise | **homoscedastic**, heteroscedastic, heavy-tailed |\\n| sparsity | **sparse**, dense |\\n\\nPer-axis samples: 2 secondary cells (one displacing canonical per axis) × 1 representative `(K_true, n, function)` combination = 6 cells × 5 algorithms × 50 seeds = **1 500 algorithm-fits**. Seed count reduced from 100 to 50 to stay inside budget (see §9).\\n\\n### 4.3 Out-of-scope extension cell (flagged, optional)\\n\\n| Cell | `p` | `K_true` | function | note |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| E1 | 10 | 3 | linear-Gaussian | Identifiability caveat: in linear-Gaussian, factor models with `K ≥ 2` are identifiable only up to rotation; we initialize `Z` with PCA so labels are calibrated to the data-implied subspace. Results reported with explicit warning. |\\n\\nIf computational slack is exhausted, E1 is dropped.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Baselines and oracles\\n\\nAll baselines and oracles produce outputs in the same DAG-on-observed space as CAMUV unless otherwise noted; FCI's PAG is mapped to a projected DAG only as a secondary comparator with explicit conversion (locked U5).\\n\\n| Algorithm | Output type | Justification |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **PC** (constraint-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Naive baseline: ignores latents; expected to mis-orient near colliders caused by latent common causes. |\\n| **GES** (score-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Score-based counterpart to PC; controls for the score-vs-constraint dimension. |\\n| **FCI** (constraint-based, latent-aware via PAG) | PAG over observed | Latent-aware baseline. PAG is *converted* to a DAG-on-observed for primary comparison by keeping only edges present in the PAG and orienting by majority PAG-mark rule; conversion loss is reported separately. PAG-native metrics (e.g., adjacency F1 on PAG) reported as **secondary**. |\\n| **Random-graph baseline** | random DAG-on-obs | Floor reference. |\\n| **Oracle-PC-with-truth-latents** | DAG-on-obs | Ceiling reference: reveals upper bound under perfect latent observability. |\\n\\nWe do **not** include a re-implemented deep latent causal discovery method as a baseline; their inclusion would require a chosen reference implementation, which conflicts with the locked decision to avoid asserting a specific CAMUV reference (symmetry: if CAMUV is abstract here, so are its closest neighbors).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Metrics\\n\\n### 6.1 Primary (projected observational DAG; locked U5)\\n- **Skeleton F1** on `G_true` vs `Ĝ` over observed-variable pairs.\\n- **Orientation accuracy** on triples where the ground-truth orientation is unambiguous in the projected DAG.\\n\\n### 6.2 Secondary\\n- **Latent-cardinality error** `|K̂ − K_true|`.\\n- **Latent-membership ARI / NMI** (for `K_true ≥ 1`) on per-observed-variable latent-confounder assignment.\\n- **PAG-native F1** for FCI only, reported alongside (not intermingled with) primary DAG F1.\\n\\n### 6.3 Tertiary (robustness, not headline)\\n- **Convergence rate** of CAMUV (fraction of seeds reaching `tol` before `max_iter`).\\n- **Hyperparameter sensitivity**: F1 spread across the grid in §2.5 at the canonical operating cell.\\n\\n### 6.4 Statistical calibration\\nFor each metric × cell, report mean and 95% bootstrap CI (BCa) over seeds. CAMUV-vs-baseline contrasts reported as paired deltas (same seed) with CIs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Controls\\n\\n### 7.1 Negative control\\n- **Permute-target DGP**: shuffle each column of `D` independently, breaking every latent and most observed edges while preserving marginals. CAMUV and baselines should drop to floor; deviation indicates leakage.\\n\\n### 7.2 Identifiability controls\\n- **Latent-leakage minimum** (≥ 3 observed children per latent; documented per cell). Cells violating this are excluded from the headline regime map.\\n- **`K_max ≥ K_true`**. If `K_max < K_true`, the cell is flagged non-identifiable for CAMUV-A/B and reported separately, not aggregated.\\n- For linear-Gaussian with `K_true ≥ 2`, factor-model rotations imply label non-identifiability; we initialize `Z` with PCA and report ARI modulo best permutation matching.\\n\\n### 7.3 Sample-size sweep control\\nThe headline battery itself spans `n ∈ {200, 1000, 10000}` precisely to expose the **phase-transition** in `n` at which CAMUV gains over FCI emerge and saturate.\\n\\n### 7.4 Runtime / parallelism control\\n- Each algorithm × cell × seed runs as an independent job.\\n- Parallelism across seeds, with target ≤ 24 concurrent workers (matches public_task.environment).\\n- Wall-clock budget ≤ 4 h per cell; total ≤ 200 single-core-hours (locked U7).\\n\\n### 7.5 Hyperparameter-selection control\\nDefault operating point chosen by 5-fold CV on a 2-cell held-out DGP sample **before** the headline battery is run. The same point is reused across all cells, so battery timings do not contaminate with per-cell tuning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Analysis plan\\n\\n### 8.1 Aggregation\\nMixed-effects model on per-seed metrics:\\n\\n```\\nmetric_ijk = β0\\n           + β_method[m]                       # CAMUV-A, CAMUV-B, PC, GES, FCI, oracle-PC\\n           + β_K[K_true]\\n           + β_n[n]\\n           + β_func[function]\\n           + (β_method × β_K)[m, K]            # headline interaction\\n           + (β_method × β_func)[m, function]\\n           + u_cell                           # random intercept per (cell, seed-block)\\n           + ε_ijk\\n```\\n\\nFit with `lme4`-style REML (or its NumPy/Python equivalent if execution occurs). Cluster-robust SE at the cell level to handle metric non-independence.\\n\\n### 8.2 Multiple-comparison correction\\nHolm correction across (i) baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-A and (ii) baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-B. Family-wise error rate controlled at α=0.05.\\n\\n### 8.3 Effect-size reporting\\n- **Primary.** Cohen's `d` and 95% CI for the CAMUV-vs-best-baseline contrast, per cell.\\n- **Headline.** ΔF1 surface over `(n, K_true, function)`, plotted as a heatmap (cells arranged in a 3 × 3 × 2 → 18 entries paneled by `function`).\\n\\n### 8.4 Decision rule (pre-registered)\\n- **Success.** CAMUV-A or CAMUV-B exceeds the best non-oracle baseline by ΔF1 ≥ 0.05 with 95% CI excluding zero in ≥ 50% of headline cells with `K_true ≥ 1` AND `n ≥ 1000`.\\n- **Mixed.** Above holds for one of `K_true ∈ {1,2}` but not the other.\\n- **Negative.** Neither scheme meets the threshold; the regime map is reported honestly and limitations (§10) are foregrounded.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility & resource estimate\\n\\n### 9.1 Algorithm-fit timing model (illustrative)\\n\\n```\\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH — NOT EXECUTED\\n# Order-of-magnitude time per (method, cell, seed). Calibrated on\\n# the canonical p=10 cell; scaled by heuristic factor in p, n.\\n\\nimport math\\n\\ndef est_seconds(p, n, method):\\n    base = {\\n        \\\"PC\\\":   0.05,\\n        \\\"GES\\\":  0.10,\\n        \\\"FCI\\\":  0.20,\\n        \\\"CAMUV-A\\\": 0.50,   # ~10x PC for inner loop + augmentation\\n        \\\"CAMUV-B\\\": 0.40,\\n        \\\"Oracle-PC\\\": 0.05,\\n    }[method]\\n    return base * (p / 10.0) * math.log2(max(n, 2)) / 10.0\\n```\\n\\nFor headline (p=10): ≈ 1.35 s × 100 seeds × 5 methods × 18 cells = ~6 075 s ≈ **1.7 core-hours** per headline battery if executed sequentially.\\nSecondary (p up to 20): ~1.7× cost ⇒ ~**2.6 core-hours**.\\nPermutation controls (one extra seed-equivalent per cell): ~**0.3 core-hours**.\\n\\n### 9.2 Budget (locked U7)\\n\\n| Item | Estimated core-hours |\\n|---|---|\\n| Headline 18 cells | 1.7 |\\n| Secondary 6 cells | 2.6 |\\n| Negative-control permutations | 0.3 |\\n| Hyperparameter inner CV (pre-battery) | 1.0 |\\n| Overhead / re-runs / analysis | 4.4 |\\n| **Total** | **≈ 10 core-hours** |\\n\\nThis is well below the 200 single-core-hour cap, leaving substantial slack for (i) replication runs, (ii) extension cell E1 (capped at 50 seeds), and (iii) hyperparameter sensitivity scans.\\n\\nWall-clock: at ≤ 24 concurrent workers, the headline battery finishes in ≈ 10 / 24 hours × 100/5 ≈ a few hours including overhead; well within the ≤ 4 h-per-cell allowance when seeds are parallelized.\\n\\n### 9.3 Software prerequisites\\nThe bubblewrap environment has no scientific libraries; execution would require installing at minimum `numpy`, `scipy`, `networkx`, `scikit-learn`, `pandas`, plus per-method implementations. This dependency is documented; **no installation has been performed for this design task**. Pure-Python re-implementation is feasible for PC/GES/FCI; CAMUV-A/B re-implementation is the largest source of effort and a known confounder.\\n\\n```\\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH — NOT EXECUTED\\n# Dependency installation would be a prerequisite to execution.\\n# Example (not run):\\n#   pip install numpy scipy networkx scikit-learn pandas\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions & limitations (locked)\\n\\n### 10.1 Locked assumptions (A1–A6)\\n- **A1.** CAMUV is defined abstractly as a latent-aware causal-discovery procedure; **CAMUV-A** (augmented-variable search) and **CAMUV-B** (factor-residualization loop) are two concrete instantiations documented side-by-side throughout the design.\\n- **A2.** Evaluation target is synthetic; the regime map is the answer.\\n- **A3.** `K_max ≤ 3`; headline regime map covers `K_true ∈ {0, 1, 2}`; extension to `K_true ≥ 3` is out-of-scope for the default battery (cell E1 optional, with explicit identifiability caveat).\\n- **A4.** Reported graph is the projected DAG on observed variables; latent structure is a secondary side-product.\\n- **A5.** No preinstalled scientific libraries; execution would require installation or from-scratch re-implementation, the latter adding a confounder that will be flagged.\\n- **A6.** Design-only deliverable; no executed experiment is claimed.\\n\\n### 10.2 Out-of-scope items (locked U2, U3, U4)\\n- Discrete-only or mixed-type data (continuous only).\\n- `K_true ≥ 3` as a default condition (only flagged extension).\\n- Post-nonlinear and discrete functional classes.\\n- Time-series observations, cyclic SCMs, measurement error.\\n- Re-implementation-versus-published-baseline confounders (§9.3).\\n\\n### 10.3 Limitations\\n- Identifiability of `Z`'s labeling is not absolute for `K_true ≥ 2` linear-Gaussian; we report best-permutation ARI, not raw ARI.\\n- FCI's PAG → DAG conversion loses information; the loss is reported as a secondary metric to keep primary-metric purity (locked U5).\\n- Single canonical `(p, sparsity, noise)` anchors the regime map; secondary battery probes generalization but cannot enumerate all combinations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Threats to validity & mitigations\\n\\n| Threat | Direction | Mitigation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Algorithm re-implementation confounder** (no preinstalled libraries ⇒ CAMUV and baselines coded from scratch) | Could bias either direction | Use ≥ 2 independent re-implementations for spot-check cells; report agreement; default to the most conservative claim supported by either. |\\n| **Hyperparameter over-tuning on the headline cells** | Inflates CAMUV's reported performance | Freeze hyperparameters via 5-fold CV on a held-out 2-cell mini-battery **before** headline runs (§7.5). |\\n| **DGP family bias** | Inflates CAMUV under chosen function class | Use ≥ 2 functional classes, ≥ 3 noise regimes, ≥ 2 sparsities. Headline generalizes within scope (§10.2); out-of-scope caveats listed. |\\n| **Identifiability violations silently absorbed** | Reports a false `K̂` / membership | Leakage rule (≥ 3 observed children/latent); flag cells where it fails; restrict headline to compliant cells. |\\n| **Metric mixing across output types (DAG vs PAG)** | False comparability | Locked U5: DAG-F1 is primary on projected DAG; PAG-native metrics are secondary and explicitly separated. |\\n| **Multiple comparisons across cells × baselines** | Inflated false-positive rate | Holm correction across CAMUV-vs-baseline contrasts within family (§8.2). |\\n| **Single-seed fragility** | High variance for small `n` | ≥ 100 seeds per headline cell; bootstrap CIs; pre-registered decision rules (§8.4). |\\n| **Latent label non-identifiability at `K_true ≥ 2`** | ARI mis-reports performance | Best-permutation ARI; rotation-invariant metrics (e.g., subspace distance) reported alongside. |\\n| **Wall-clock cliff on oversize cells** | Dropped cells | ≤ 4 h wall-clock per cell cap; cells exceeding it are demoted to a \\\"skipped\\\" status in the regime map rather than silently truncated. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. References / literature anchors\\n\\n**Policy.** Per the locked directive, CAMUV is *not* cited as a specific paper, version, or reference implementation. The following named anchors are well-known causal-discovery algorithm **classes** referenced only to disambiguate terms used in the design; specific papers should be added by the reader if and when a reference implementation is chosen.\\n\\n- **PC algorithm** — constraint-based causal discovery over observed variables (Spirtes–Glymour–Scheines line of work, widely catalogued in modern causal-discovery textbooks).\\n- **GES** — Greedy Equivalence Search, score-based equivalence-class search (Chickering line of work).\\n- **FCI** — Fast Causal Inference, constraint-based discovery that returns a PAG and is intended to handle latent common causes (Spirtes line of work; Zhang's later refinement is widely cited for soundness/completeness refinement).\\n- **Linear-Gaussian factor models and PCA initialization** — standard textbook material; no specific anchor cited.\\n- **ARI / NMI** — Hubert & Arabie (1985) for ARI; standard information-theoretic definitions for NMI.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix A. Illustrative-only real-data sketch (optional, clearly labeled)\\n\\nIf, at the researcher's discretion, a real-world observational illustration is desirable without external assets, the very minimum requirement is a dataset that ships *with a Python package already installed or constructed from built-in sources*. No such dataset is mandated; providing one would require explicit researcher direction. **No dataset is included in this design**, consistent with locked U6. If later added, it must be reported in a separate appendix section labeled `ILLUSTRATIVE — REAL-DATA APPENDIX (NOT EXECUTED)`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix B. Headline deliverable checklist\\n\\nThe design produces (without claiming execution):\\n\\n1. The markdown document you are reading, at `/workspace/experiment_design.md`.\\n2. A pre-registered decision rule (§8.4).\\n3. A locked assumption manifest (§10.1).\\n4. A feasibility proof (§9) showing the design fits inside locked budgets.\\n5. An out-of-scope manifest (§10.2) honest about what is *not* being claimed.\\n6. **No** executed experiment; **no** claimed performance numbers.\\nDESIGN_EOF\\necho '--- write complete ---'\\nwc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1948e1e6-6362-4cd1-946d-280320407d57", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
