ocked)\\nNo external reference was supplied for CAMUV. Per the researcher's locked resolution (U1), we adopt a **parameterized abstract model** and instantiate it concretely as two schemes, **CAMUV-A** and **CAMUV-B**, used consistently throughout:\\n\\n- **CAMUV-A \\u2014 augmented-variable search.** Treat latent factors as additional unmeasured nodes and run a causal discovery procedure on the *augmented* set, restricted so latents are never conditioned on directly. Refine `(K, Z)` by a penalized score.\\n- **CAMUV-B \\u2014 factor-residualization loop.** Alternately fit a factor model on `X`, regress residuals against observed variables for an \\\"observed-only\\\" graph, then update factors from residual covariances until convergence.\\n\\nBoth schemes consume the same inputs and produce the same outputs; they differ in how `(graph, Z, K)` are searched.\\n\\n### 2.2 Inputs (uniform)\\n| Symbol | Meaning | Default |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `D` in R^{n x p} | Observational data over p observed variables | required |\\n| `K_max` | Maximum candidate latent count | <= 3 (locked, A3) |\\n| subroutine (Scheme A: {PC, GES}; Scheme B: {factor-model class, observed-only CD}) | Inner discovery routine | ablated |\\n| `alpha` | CI test significance level | {0.01, 0.05} |\\n| `init in {random, PCA, k-means}` | Factor initialization | ablated |\\n| `tol`, `max_iter` | Convergence | 1e-4, 50 |\\n\\n### 2.3 Outputs (uniform)\\n- `G_hat`: estimated DAG on observed variables (skeleton + orientation).\\n- `K_hat`: estimated latent count.\\n- `M_hat`: per-observed-variable latent-membership assignment.\\n\\n### 2.4 Pseudocode\\n\\n```\\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH \\u2014 NOT EXECUTED\\n# Abstract CAMUV procedure; both schemes share inputs/outputs.\\n\\ndef camuv(D, p, K_max,\\n          scheme,                 # \\\"A\\\" or \\\"B\\\"\\n          inner,                  # inner CD routine\\n          alpha=0.05,\\n          init=\\\"pca\\\",\\n          tol=1e-4, max_iter=50):\\n    n = D.shape[0]\\n\\n    # 1. Initialize latent representation Z, K = 1\\n    K = 1\\n    Z = init_latents(D, K=K, method=init)\\n\\n    G_hat, score_prev = None, -inf\\n\\n    for it in range(max_iter):\\n        # 2a. Scheme A: search on the augmented (X U Z)\\n        if scheme == \\\"A\\\":\\n            G_tilde = inner(D, Z, alpha=alpha)        # DAG on X U Z\\n            G_hat   = project_observed(G_tilde, p)     # drop latent nodes\\n            Z_new   = refine_Z_from_augmented(D, Z, G_tilde)\\n\\n        # 2b. Scheme B: factor-residualization loop\\n        else:\\n            Lambda, eps = factor_model(D, Z)           # X ~= Lambda Z + eps\\n            G_hat      = inner_observed(D - eps, alpha)\\n            Z_new, K   = update_factors(D, G_hat, eps, K, K_max)\\n\\n        score = penalized_fit(D, Z_new, G_hat, K)\\n        if abs(score - score_prev) < tol:\\n            break\\n        Z, score_prev = Z_new, score\\n\\n    return G_hat, K, latent_membership(K, Z, D)\\n```\\n\\nThe above is a **specification skeleton only**. Concrete choices for `inner`, `init_latents`, `factor_model`, `refine_Z_from_augmented`, `update_factors`, `penalized_fit` are intentionally left parametric to remain faithful to the locked decision not to assert any single paper/version.\\n\\n### 2.5 Hyperparameter grid\\n\\n| Hyperparameter | Levels |\\n|---|---|\\n| `scheme` | {A, B} |\\n| `inner` (Scheme A) | {PC with Fisher-z, GES with BIC-Gauss} |\\n| `inner_observed` (Scheme B) | {PC with Fisher-z, GES with BIC-Gauss} |\\n| `init` | {PCA, random normal, k-means on rows of D} |\\n| `K_max` | {1, 2, 3} |\\n| `alpha` | {0.01, 0.05} |\\n| `tol`, `max_iter` | fixed (see 2.2) |\\n\\nDefault operating point chosen via inner cross-validated selection on a held-out DGPs subset, then frozen before the headline battery runs to avoid double-dipping.\\n\\nEOF1\\necho '--- part 1 written ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.016, \"output\": \"--- part 1 written ---\\n106 /workspace/experiment_design.md\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "e31c8852-e4fe-476a-8a6e-1d2ffc4ee8da", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "650925a4-a26f-4d06-a9c2-13e5c2103366", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:05:43.529886", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c76d3258-dadf-4e57-81d2-7b5244ab11b9", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF2'\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data-generating process (DGP) specification\n\n### 3.1 SCM form\nEach DGP cell generates a DAG on `p_obs + K_true` nodes (latents included in the ground truth only, never observed), then samples from\n\n```\nX_j = f_j( Pa_obs(X_j) U Pa_lat(X_j) ) + eps_j ,   j = 1, ..., p\n```\n\nwhere:\n- `Pa_obs(X_j)` is a subset of observed variables; `Pa_lat(X_j)` is a subset of latent variables.\n- Edge weights are drawn i.i.d. from `Uniform([-1.5, -0.3] U [0.3, 1.5])` (avoid trivial-near-zero weights).\n- Functional class:\n  - **linear-Gaussian**: `f_j` linear; `eps_j ~ N(0, sigma_j^2)`.\n  - **nonlinear-additive**: `f_j(X) = Sum_k phi_k(X_k)` with `phi_k` drawn i.i.d. from {`tanh(beta *)`, `sin(beta *)`, polynomial degree <= 3}; `eps_j` additive Gaussian.\n- Noise regimes:\n  - **homoscedastic Gaussian**: `sigma_j == 1`.\n  - **heteroscedastic**: `sigma_j ~ Uniform(0.5, 2.0)`.\n  - **heavy-tailed**: `eps_j ~ Student-t(nu=3)` rescaled to unit variance.\n- Sparsity:\n  - **sparse**: expected degree per observed node ~ 1.5.\n  - **dense**: expected degree ~ 3.\n\n### 3.2 Latent leakage rule\nEvery latent has >= 3 observed children (otherwise the latent is empirically non-identifiable and the cell is flagged).\n\n### 3.3 Ground-truth recording (per cell, per seed)\n- `G_true`: adjacency on observed nodes only (projected DAG).\n- `O_true`: orientation matrix on observable triples.\n- `K_true`, per-variable latent-membership matrix `L_true` (latent-confounder sets).\n- `parent_weights`, `sigma_j` (for reproducibility and sanity).\n\n### 3.4 Out-of-scope DGP variants (explicitly listed)\n- Discrete-only or mixed-type data (locked U2).\n- Post-nonlinear functional class (locked U4).\n- Time series (`n` here is i.i.d. samples, not time-indexed).\n- Cyclic / equilibrium SCMs.\n- Measurement error / proxy-only instruments.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Factorial battery table\n\nThe headline regime map uses a focused 3 x 3 x 2 grid over `(n, K_true, function)` at canonical `(p=10, sparsity=sparse, noise=homoscedastic, K_max=3)`. A secondary battery varies the remaining factors at reduced seed count.\n\n### 4.1 Headline battery (regime map)\n\n| Cell | p | K_true | function | noise | sparsity | n | seeds |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\n| H2 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\n| H3 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\n| H4 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\n| H5 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\n| H6 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\n| H7 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\n| H8 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\n| H9 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\n| H10 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\n| H11 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\n| H12 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\n| H13 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\n| H14 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\n| H15 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\n| H16 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\n| H17 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\n| H18 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\n\nTotal headline runs: 18 cells x 5 algorithms x 100 seeds = **9 000 algorithm-fits**.\n\n### 4.2 Secondary battery (smaller sub-grid; identifies where the headline generalizes)\n\n| Axis varied | Levels (canonical in **bold**) |\n|---|---|\n| p | **{5, 10}, 20** |\n| noise | **homoscedastic**, heteroscedastic, heavy-tailed |\n| sparsity | **sparse**, dense |\n\nPer-axis samples: 2 secondary cells (one displacing canonical per axis) at the (K_true, n, function) = (1, 1000, linear-Gaussian) reference point = 6 cells x 5 algorithms x 50 seeds = **1 500 algorithm-fits**. Seed count reduced from 100 to 50 to stay inside budget (see section 9).\n\n### 4.3 Out-of-scope extension cell (flagged, optional)\n\n| Cell | p | K_true | function | note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| E1 | 10 | 3 | linear-Gaussian | Identifiability caveat: in linear-Gaussian, factor models with K >= 2 are identifiable only up to rotation; we initialize `Z` with PCA so labels are calibrated to the data-implied subspace. Results reported with explicit warning. |\n\nIf computational slack is exhausted, E1 is dropped.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Baselines and oracles\n\nAll baselines and oracles produce outputs in the same DAG-on-observed space as CAMUV unless otherwise noted; FCI's PAG is mapped to a projected DAG only as a secondary comparator with explicit conversion (locked U5).\n\n| Algorithm | Output type | Justification |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PC** (constraint-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Naive baseline: ignores latents; expected to mis-orient near colliders caused by latent common causes. |\n| **GES** (score-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Score-based counterpart to PC; controls for the score-vs-constraint dimension. |\n| **FCI** (constraint-based, latent-aware via PAG) | PAG over observed | Latent-aware baseline. PAG is *converted* to a DAG-on-observed for primary comparison by keeping only edges present in the PAG and orienting by majority PAG-mark rule; conversion loss is reported separately. PAG-native metrics (e.g., adjacency F1 on PAG) reported as **secondary**. |\n| **Random-graph baseline** | random DAG-on-obs | Floor reference. |\n| **Oracle-PC-with-truth-latents** | DAG-on-obs | Ceiling reference: reveals upper bound under perfect latent observability. |\n\nWe do **not** include a re-implemented deep latent causal-discovery method as a baseline; their inclusion would require a chosen reference implementation, which conflicts with the locked decision to avoid asserting a specific CAMUV reference (symmetry: if CAMUV is abstract here, so are its closest neighbors).\n\nEOF2\necho '--- part 2 appended ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-5f80555a-ff78-44b8-8a08-bfe8c9657149", "tool_call": {"id": "call-5f80555a-ff78-44b8-8a08-bfe8c9657149", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append design doc part 2 (sections 3-5)\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF2'\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Data-generating process (DGP) specification\\n\\n### 3.1 SCM form\\nEach DGP cell generates a DAG on `p_obs + K_true` nodes (latents included in the ground truth only, never observed), then samples from\\n\\n```\\nX_j = f_j( Pa_obs(X_j) U Pa_lat(X_j) ) + eps_j ,   j = 1, ..., p\\n```\\n\\nwhere:\\n- `Pa_obs(X_j)` is a subset of observed variables; `Pa_lat(X_j)` is a subset of latent variables.\\n- Edge weights are drawn i.i.d. from `Uniform([-1.5, -0.3] U [0.3, 1.5])` (avoid trivial-near-zero weights).\\n- Functional class:\\n  - **linear-Gaussian**: `f_j` linear; `eps_j ~ N(0, sigma_j^2)`.\\n  - **nonlinear-additive**: `f_j(X) = Sum_k phi_k(X_k)` with `phi_k` drawn i.i.d. from {`tanh(beta *)`, `sin(beta *)`, polynomial degree <= 3}; `eps_j` additive Gaussian.\\n- Noise regimes:\\n  - **homoscedastic Gaussian**: `sigma_j == 1`.\\n  - **heteroscedastic**: `sigma_j ~ Uniform(0.5, 2.0)`.\\n  - **heavy-tailed**: `eps_j ~ Student-t(nu=3)` rescaled to unit variance.\\n- Sparsity:\\n  - **sparse**: expected degree per observed node ~ 1