cked U5).\\n\\n| Algorithm | Output type | Justification |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **PC** (constraint-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Naive baseline: ignores latents; expected to mis-orient near colliders caused by latent common causes. |\\n| **GES** (score-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Score-based counterpart to PC; controls for the score-vs-constraint dimension. |\\n| **FCI** (constraint-based, latent-aware via PAG) | PAG over observed | Latent-aware baseline. PAG is *converted* to a DAG-on-observed for primary comparison by keeping only edges present in the PAG and orienting by majority PAG-mark rule; conversion loss is reported separately. PAG-native metrics (e.g., adjacency F1 on PAG) reported as **secondary**. |\\n| **Random-graph baseline** | random DAG-on-obs | Floor reference. |\\n| **Oracle-PC-with-truth-latents** | DAG-on-obs | Ceiling reference: reveals upper bound under perfect latent observability. |\\n\\nWe do **not** include a re-implemented deep latent causal-discovery method as a baseline; their inclusion would require a chosen reference implementation, which conflicts with the locked decision to avoid asserting a specific CAMUV reference (symmetry: if CAMUV is abstract here, so are its closest neighbors).\\n\\nEOF2\\necho '--- part 2 appended ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-984e3ce0-c6a0-4ded-95ff-5f57ef2bf74b", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
{"id": "b924cb62-a685-4045-9985-76587e37f5ae", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:05:43.551020", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "650925a4-a26f-4d06-a9c2-13e5c2103366", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-5f80555a-ff78-44b8-8a08-bfe8c9657149", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF2'\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Data-generating process (DGP) specification\\n\\n### 3.1 SCM form\\nEach DGP cell generates a DAG on `p_obs + K_true` nodes (latents included in the ground truth only, never observed), then samples from\\n\\n```\\nX_j = f_j( Pa_obs(X_j) U Pa_lat(X_j) ) + eps_j ,   j = 1, ..., p\\n```\\n\\nwhere:\\n- `Pa_obs(X_j)` is a subset of observed variables; `Pa_lat(X_j)` is a subset of latent variables.\\n- Edge weights are drawn i.i.d. from `Uniform([-1.5, -0.3] U [0.3, 1.5])` (avoid trivial-near-zero weights).\\n- Functional class:\\n  - **linear-Gaussian**: `f_j` linear; `eps_j ~ N(0, sigma_j^2)`.\\n  - **nonlinear-additive**: `f_j(X) = Sum_k phi_k(X_k)` with `phi_k` drawn i.i.d. from {`tanh(beta *)`, `sin(beta *)`, polynomial degree <= 3}; `eps_j` additive Gaussian.\\n- Noise regimes:\\n  - **homoscedastic Gaussian**: `sigma_j == 1`.\\n  - **heteroscedastic**: `sigma_j ~ Uniform(0.5, 2.0)`.\\n  - **heavy-tailed**: `eps_j ~ Student-t(nu=3)` rescaled to unit variance.\\n- Sparsity:\\n  - **sparse**: expected degree per observed node ~ 1.5.\\n  - **dense**: expected degree ~ 3.\\n\\n### 3.2 Latent leakage rule\\nEvery latent has >= 3 observed children (otherwise the latent is empirically non-identifiable and the cell is flagged).\\n\\n### 3.3 Ground-truth recording (per cell, per seed)\\n- `G_true`: adjacency on observed nodes only (projected DAG).\\n- `O_true`: orientation matrix on observable triples.\\n- `K_true`, per-variable latent-membership matrix `L_true` (latent-confounder sets).\\n- `parent_weights`, `sigma_j` (for reproducibility and sanity).\\n\\n### 3.4 Out-of-scope DGP variants (explicitly listed)\\n- Discrete-only or mixed-type data (locked U2).\\n- Post-nonlinear functional class (locked U4).\\n- Time series (`n` here is i.i.d. samples, not time-indexed).\\n- Cyclic / equilibrium SCMs.\\n- Measurement error / proxy-only instruments.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Factorial battery table\\n\\nThe headline regime map uses a focused 3 x 3 x 2 grid over `(n, K_true, function)` at canonical `(p=10, sparsity=sparse, noise=homoscedastic, K_max=3)`. A secondary battery varies the remaining factors at reduced seed count.\\n\\n### 4.1 Headline battery (regime map)\\n\\n| Cell | p | K_true | function | noise | sparsity | n | seeds |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| H1 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H2 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H3 | 10 | 0 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H4 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H5 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H6 | 10 | 1 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H7 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H8 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H9 | 10 | 2 | linear-Gaussian | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H10 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H11 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H12 | 10 | 0 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H13 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H14 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H15 | 10 | 1 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n| H16 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 200 | 100 |\\n| H17 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 1 000 | 100 |\\n| H18 | 10 | 2 | nonlinear-additive | homoscedastic | sparse | 10 000 | 100 |\\n\\nTotal headline runs: 18 cells x 5 algorithms x 100 seeds = **9 000 algorithm-fits**.\\n\\n### 4.2 Secondary battery (smaller sub-grid; identifies where the headline generalizes)\\n\\n| Axis varied | Levels (canonical in **bold**) |\\n|---|---|\\n| p | **{5, 10}, 20** |\\n| noise | **homoscedastic**, heteroscedastic, heavy-tailed |\\n| sparsity | **sparse**, dense |\\n\\nPer-axis samples: 2 secondary cells (one displacing canonical per axis) at the (K_true, n, function) = (1, 1000, linear-Gaussian) reference point = 6 cells x 5 algorithms x 50 seeds = **1 500 algorithm-fits**. Seed count reduced from 100 to 50 to stay inside budget (see section 9).\\n\\n### 4.3 Out-of-scope extension cell (flagged, optional)\\n\\n| Cell | p | K_true | function | note |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| E1 | 10 | 3 | linear-Gaussian | Identifiability caveat: in linear-Gaussian, factor models with K >= 2 are identifiable only up to rotation; we initialize `Z` with PCA so labels are calibrated to the data-implied subspace. Results reported with explicit warning. |\\n\\nIf computational slack is exhausted, E1 is dropped.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Baselines and oracles\\n\\nAll baselines and oracles produce outputs in the same DAG-on-observed space as CAMUV unless otherwise noted; FCI's PAG is mapped to a projected DAG only as a secondary comparator with explicit conversion (locked U5).\\n\\n| Algorithm | Output type | Justification |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **PC** (constraint-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Naive baseline: ignores latents; expected to mis-orient near colliders caused by latent common causes. |\\n| **GES** (score-based, observed only) | DAG-on-observed | Score-based counterpart to PC; controls for the score-vs-constraint dimension. |\\n| **FCI** (constraint-based, latent-aware via PAG) | PAG over observed | Latent-aware baseline. PAG is *converted* to a DAG-on-observed for primary comparison by keeping only edges present in the PAG and orienting by majority PAG-mark rule; conversion loss is reported separately. PAG-native metrics (e.g., adjacency F1 on PAG) reported as **secondary**. |\\n| **Random-graph baseline** | random DAG-on-obs | Floor reference. |\\n| **Oracle-PC-with-truth-latents** | DAG-on-obs | Ceiling reference: reveals upper bound under perfect latent observability. |\\n\\nWe do **not** include a re-implemented deep latent causal-discovery method as a baseline; their inclusion would require a chosen reference implementation, which conflicts with the locked decision to avoid asserting a specific CAMUV reference (symmetry: if CAMUV is abstract here, so are its closest neighbors).\\n\\nEOF2\\necho '--- part 2 appended ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.017, \"output\": \"--- part 2 appended ---\\n213 /workspace/experiment_design.md\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "650925a4-a26f-4d06-a9c2-13e5c2103366", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 24}
{"id": "c57b662b-bf4b-4bc5-81d3-56794234110b", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:06:03.537357", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b924cb62-a685-4045-9985-76587e37f5ae", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF3'\n\n---\n\n## 6. Metrics\n\n### 6.1 Primary (projected observational DAG; locked U5)\n- **Skeleton F1** on `G_true` vs `G_hat` over observed-variable pairs.\n- **Orientation accuracy** on triples where the ground-truth orientation is unambiguous in the projected DAG.\n\n### 6.2 Secondary\n- **Latent-cardinality error** `|K_hat - K_true|`.\n- **Latent-membership ARI / NMI** (for K_true >= 1) on per-observed-variable latent-confounder assignment; ARI is computed *modulo best permutation matching* to handle the labeling non-identifiability documented in section 11.\n- **PAG-native F1** for FCI only, reported alongside (not intermingled with) primary DAG F1, per locked U5.\n\n### 6.3 Tertiary (robustness, not headline)\n- **Convergence rate** of CAMUV (fraction of seeds reaching `tol` before `max_iter`).\n- **Hyperparameter sensitivity**: F1 spread across the grid in section 2.5 at the canonical operating cell.\n\n### 6.4 Statistical calibration\nFor each metric x cell, report mean and 95% bootstrap CI (BCa) over seeds. CAMUV-vs-baseline contrasts reported as paired deltas (same seed) with CIs.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls\n\n### 7.1 Negative control\n- **Permute-target DGP**: shuffle each column of `D` independently, breaking every latent and most observed edges while preserving marginals. CAMUV and baselines should drop to floor; deviation indicates leakage.\n\n### 7.2 Identifiability controls\n- **Latent-leakage minimum** (>= 3 observed children per latent; documented per cell). Cells violating this are excluded from the headline regime map.\n- **`K_max >= K_true`**. If `K_max < K_true`, the cell is flagged non-identifiable for CAMUV-A/B and reported separately, not aggregated.\n- For linear-Gaussian with K_true >= 2, factor-model rotations imply label non-identifiability; we initialize `Z` with PCA and report ARI modulo best permutation matching.\n\n### 7.3 Sample-size sweep control\nThe headline battery itself spans `n in {200, 1000, 10000}` precisely to expose the **phase-transition** in `n` at which CAMUV gains over FCI emerge and saturate.\n\n### 7.4 Runtime / parallelism control\n- Each algorithm x cell x seed runs as an independent job.\n- Parallelism across seeds, with target <= 24 concurrent workers (matches public_task.environment).\n- Wall-clock budget <= 4 h per cell; total <= 200 single-core-hours (locked U7).\n\n### 7.5 Hyperparameter-selection control\nDefault operating point chosen by 5-fold CV on a 2-cell held-out DGP sample **before** the headline battery is run. The same point is reused across all cells, so battery timings do not contaminate with per-cell tuning.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Analysis plan\n\n### 8.1 Aggregation\nMixed-effects model on per-seed metrics:\n\n```\nmetric_ijk = beta_0\n           + beta_method[m]                       # CAMUV-A, CAMUV-B, PC, GES, FCI, oracle-PC\n           + beta_K[K_true]\n           + beta_n[n]\n           + beta_func[function]\n           + (beta_method x beta_K)[m, K]         # headline interaction\n           + (beta_method x beta_func)[m, function]\n           + u_cell                               # random intercept per cell\n           + eps_ijk\n```\n\nFit with REML (or its Python/NumPy equivalent if execution occurs). Cluster-robust SE at the cell level to handle non-independence of metrics within a cell.\n\n### 8.2 Multiple-comparison correction\nHolm correction across (i) baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-A and (ii) baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-B. Family-wise error rate controlled at alpha=0.05.\n\n### 8.3 Effect-size reporting\n- **Primary.** Cohen's `d` and 95% CI for the CAMUV-vs-best-baseline contrast, per cell.\n- **Headline.** Delta F1 surface over `(n, K_true, function)`, plotted as a heatmap (paneled by `function`, 3 x 3 cells per panel).\n\n### 8.4 Decision rule (pre-registered)\n- **Success.** CAMUV-A or CAMUV-B exceeds the best non-oracle baseline by Delta F1 >= 0.05 with 95% CI excluding zero in >= 50% of headline cells with K_true >= 1 AND n >= 1000.\n- **Mixed.** Above holds for one of `K_true in {1, 2}` but not the other.\n- **Negative.** Neither scheme meets the threshold; the regime map is reported honestly and limitations (section 10) are foregrounded.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Feasibility & resource estimate\n\n### 9.1 Algorithm-fit timing model (illustrative)\n\nPseudo-estimate for at-each-(method, cell, seed) wall time, calibrated on the canonical p=10 cell:\n\n```\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH -- NOT EXECUTED\n# Order-of-magnitude time per (method, cell, seed). Calibrated on\n# the canonical p=10 cell; scaled by heuristic factor in p, n.\n\nimport math\n\ndef est_seconds(p, n, method):\n    base = {\n        \"PC\":          0.05,\n        \"GES\":         0.10,\n        \"FCI\":         0.20,\n        \"CAMUV-A\":     0.50,   # ~10x PC for inner loop + augmentation\n        \"CAMUV-B\":     0.40,\n        \"Oracle-PC\":   0.05,\n    }[method]\n    return base * (p / 10.0) * math.log2(max(n, 2)) / 10.0\n```\n\nFor the headline battery (p=10): ~1.35 s x 100 seeds x 5 methods x 18 cells = ~6 075 s ~ **1.7 core-hours** if executed sequentially.\nSecondary (p up to 20): ~1.7x cost implies ~**2.6 core-hours**.\nPermutation controls (one extra seed-equivalent per cell): ~**0.3 core-hours**.\n\n### 9.2 Budget (locked U7)\n\n| Item | Estimated core-hours |\n|---|---|\n| Headline 18 cells | 1.7 |\n| Secondary 6 cells | 2.6 |\n| Negative-control permutations | 0.3 |\n| Hyperparameter inner CV (pre-battery) | 1.0 |\n| Overhead / re-runs / analysis | 4.4 |\n| **Total** | **~ 10 core-hours** |\n\nThis is well below the 200 single-core-hour cap, leaving substantial slack for (i) replication runs, (ii) extension cell E1 (capped at 50 seeds), and (iii) hyperparameter sensitivity scans.\n\nWall-clock budget: at up to 24 concurrent workers, the headline battery finishes in a few hours including overhead, well within the <= 4 h-per-cell allowance when seeds are parallelized.\n\n### 9.3 Software prerequisites\nThe bubblewrap environment has no scientific libraries; execution would require installing at minimum `numpy`, `scipy`, `networkx`, `scikit-learn`, `pandas`, plus per-method implementations. This dependency is documented; **no installation has been performed for this design task**. Pure-Python re-implementation is feasible for PC/GES/FCI; CAMUV-A/B re-implementation is the largest source of effort and a known confounder.\n\n```\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH -- NOT EXECUTED\n# Dependency installation would be a prerequisite to execution.\n# Example (not run in this task):\n#   pip install numpy scipy networkx scikit-learn pandas\n```\n\nEOF3\necho '--- part 3 appended ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-c3308cb6-39c1-4b73-92dc-5cb5550d32f1", "tool_call": {"id": "call-c3308cb6-39c1-4b73-92dc-5cb5550d32f1", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append design doc part 3 (sections 6-9)\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF3'\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Metrics\\n\\n### 6.1 Primary (projected observational DAG; locked U5)\\n- **Skeleton F1** on `G_true` vs `G_hat` over observed-variable pairs.\\n- **Orientation accuracy** on triples where the ground-truth orientation is unambiguous in the projected DAG.\\n\\n### 6.2 Secondary\\n- **Latent-cardinality error** `|K_hat - K_true|`.\\n- **Latent-membership ARI / NMI** (for K_true >= 1) on per-observed-variable latent-confounder assignment; ARI is computed *modulo best permutation matching* to handle the labeling non-identifiability documented in section 11.\\n- **PAG-native F1** for FCI only, reported alongside (not intermingled with) primary DAG F1,