headline regime map.\\n- **`K_max >= K_true`**. If `K_max < K_true`, the cell is flagged non-identifiable for CAMUV-A/B and reported separately, not aggregated.\\n- For linear-Gaussian with K_true >= 2, factor-model rotations imply label non-identifiability; we initialize `Z` with PCA and report ARI modulo best permutation matching.\\n\\n### 7.3 Sample-size sweep control\\nThe headline battery itself spans `n in {200, 1000, 10000}` precisely to expose the **phase-transition** in `n` at which CAMUV gains over FCI emerge and saturate.\\n\\n### 7.4 Runtime / parallelism control\\n- Each algorithm x cell x seed runs as an independent job.\\n- Parallelism across seeds, with target <= 24 concurrent workers (matches public_task.environment).\\n- Wall-clock budget <= 4 h per cell; total <= 200 single-core-hours (locked U7).\\n\\n### 7.5 Hyperparameter-selection control\\nDefault operating point chosen by 5-fold CV on a 2-cell held-out DGP sample **before** the headline battery is run. The same point is reused across all cells, so battery timings do not contaminate with per-cell tuning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Analysis plan\\n\\n### 8.1 Aggregation\\nMixed-effects model on per-seed metrics:\\n\\n```\\nmetric_ijk = beta_0\\n           + beta_method[m]                       # CAMUV-A, CAMUV-B, PC, GES, FCI, oracle-PC\\n           + beta_K[K_true]\\n           + beta_n[n]\\n           + beta_func[function]\\n           + (beta_method x beta_K)[m, K]         # headline interaction\\n           + (beta_method x beta_func)[m, function]\\n           + u_cell                               # random intercept per cell\\n           + eps_ijk\\n```\\n\\nFit with REML (or its Python/NumPy equivalent if execution occurs). Cluster-robust SE at the cell level to handle non-independence of metrics within a cell.\\n\\n### 8.2 Multiple-comparison correction\\nHolm correction across (i) baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-A and (ii) baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-B. Family-wise error rate controlled at alpha=0.05.\\n\\n### 8.3 Effect-size reporting\\n- **Primary.** Cohen's `d` and 95% CI for the CAMUV-vs-best-baseline contrast, per cell.\\n- **Headline.** Delta F1 surface over `(n, K_true, function)`, plotted as a heatmap (paneled by `function`, 3 x 3 cells per panel).\\n\\n### 8.4 Decision rule (pre-registered)\\n- **Success.** CAMUV-A or CAMUV-B exceeds the best non-oracle baseline by Delta F1 >= 0.05 with 95% CI excluding zero in >= 50% of headline cells with K_true >= 1 AND n >= 1000.\\n- **Mixed.** Above holds for one of `K_true in {1, 2}` but not the other.\\n- **Negative.** Neither scheme meets the threshold; the regime map is reported honestly and limitations (section 10) are foregrounded.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility & resource estimate\\n\\n### 9.1 Algorithm-fit timing model (illustrative)\\n\\nPseudo-estimate for at-each-(method, cell, seed) wall time, calibrated on the canonical p=10 cell:\\n\\n```\\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH -- NOT EXECUTED\\n# Order-of-magnitude time per (method, cell, seed). Calibrated on\\n# the canonical p=10 cell; scaled by heuristic factor in p, n.\\n\\nimport math\\n\\ndef est_seconds(p, n, method):\\n    base = {\\n        \\\"PC\\\":          0.05,\\n        \\\"GES\\\":         0.10,\\n        \\\"FCI\\\":         0.20,\\n        \\\"CAMUV-A\\\":     0.50,   # ~10x PC for inner loop + augmentation\\n        \\\"CAMUV-B\\\":     0.40,\\n        \\\"Oracle-PC\\\":   0.05,\\n    }[method]\\n    return base * (p / 10.0) * math.log2(max(n, 2)) / 10.0\\n```\\n\\nFor the headline battery (p=10): ~1.35 s x 100 seeds x 5 methods x 18 cells = ~6 075 s ~ **1.7 core-hours** if executed sequentially.\\nSecondary (p up to 20): ~1.7x cost implies ~**2.6 core-hours**.\\nPermutation controls (one extra seed-equivalent per cell): ~**0.3 core-hours**.\\n\\n### 9.2 Budget (locked U7)\\n\\n| Item | Estimated core-hours |\\n|---|---|\\n| Headline 18 cells | 1.7 |\\n| Secondary 6 cells | 2.6 |\\n| Negative-control permutations | 0.3 |\\n| Hyperparameter inner CV (pre-battery) | 1.0 |\\n| Overhead / re-runs / analysis | 4.4 |\\n| **Total** | **~ 10 core-hours** |\\n\\nThis is well below the 200 single-core-hour cap, leaving substantial slack for (i) replication runs, (ii) extension cell E1 (capped at 50 seeds), and (iii) hyperparameter sensitivity scans.\\n\\nWall-clock budget: at up to 24 concurrent workers, the headline battery finishes in a few hours including overhead, well within the <= 4 h-per-cell allowance when seeds are parallelized.\\n\\n### 9.3 Software prerequisites\\nThe bubblewrap environment has no scientific libraries; execution would require installing at minimum `numpy`, `scipy`, `networkx`, `scikit-learn`, `pandas`, plus per-method implementations. This dependency is documented; **no installation has been performed for this design task**. Pure-Python re-implementation is feasible for PC/GES/FCI; CAMUV-A/B re-implementation is the largest source of effort and a known confounder.\\n\\n```\\n# ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH -- NOT EXECUTED\\n# Dependency installation would be a prerequisite to execution.\\n# Example (not run in this task):\\n#   pip install numpy scipy networkx scikit-learn pandas\\n```\\n\\nEOF3\\necho '--- part 3 appended ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.016, \"output\": \"--- part 3 appended ---\\n346 /workspace/experiment_design.md\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "c57b662b-bf4b-4bc5-81d3-56794234110b", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 26}
{"id": "b9807fda-eed1-4670-ae37-0e40a68bc2b8", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:06:23.690757", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "dc3cd4f1-dad0-448c-ab2e-23c3efa4a8fd", "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF4'\n\n---\n\n## 10. Assumptions & limitations (locked)\n\n### 10.1 Locked assumptions (A1-A6)\n- **A1.** CAMUV is defined abstractly as a latent-aware causal-discovery procedure; **CAMUV-A** (augmented-variable search) and **CAMUV-B** (factor-residualization loop) are two concrete instantiations documented side-by-side throughout the design.\n- **A2.** Evaluation target is synthetic; the regime map is the answer.\n- **A3.** `K_max <= 3`; headline regime map covers `K_true in {0, 1, 2}`; extension to `K_true >= 3` is out-of-scope for the default battery (cell E1 optional, with explicit identifiability caveat).\n- **A4.** Reported graph is the projected DAG on observed variables; latent structure is a secondary side-product.\n- **A5.** No preinstalled scientific libraries; execution would require installation or from-scratch re-implementation, the latter adding a confounder that will be flagged.\n- **A6.** Design-only deliverable; no executed experiment is claimed.\n\n### 10.2 Out-of-scope items (locked U2, U3, U4)\n- Discrete-only or mixed-type data (continuous only).\n- K_true >= 3 as a default condition (only flagged extension E1).\n- Post-nonlinear and discrete functional classes.\n- Time-series observations, cyclic SCMs, measurement error.\n- Re-implementation-versus-published-baseline confounders (section 9.3).\n\n### 10.3 Limitations\n- Identifiability of `Z`'s labeling is not absolute for K_true >= 2 linear-Gaussian; we report best-permutation ARI, not raw ARI.\n- FCI's PAG -> DAG conversion loses information; the loss is reported as a secondary metric to keep primary-metric purity (locked U5).\n- Single canonical `(p, sparsity, noise)` anchors the regime map; secondary battery probes generalization but cannot enumerate all combinations.\n- No specific CAMUV reference is asserted; the CAMUV-A/B parameterization is an explicit working definition, not a claim about a published algorithm.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Threats to validity & mitigations\n\n| Threat | Direction | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Algorithm re-implementation confounder** (no preinstalled libraries => CAMUV and baselines coded from scratch) | Could bias either direction | Use >= 2 independent re-implementations for spot-check cells; report agreement; default to the most conservative claim supported by either. |\n| **Hyperparameter over-tuning on the headline cells** | Inflates CAMUV's reported performance | Freeze hyperparameters via 5-fold CV on a held-out 2-cell mini-battery **before** headline runs (section 7.5). |\n| **DGP family bias** | Inflates CAMUV under chosen function class | Use >= 2 functional classes, >= 3 noise regimes, >= 2 sparsities. Headline generalizes within scope (section 10.2); out-of-scope caveats listed. |\n| **Identifiability violations silently absorbed** | Reports a false `K_hat` / membership | Leakage rule (>= 3 observed children/latent); flag cells where it fails; restrict headline to compliant cells. |\n| **Metric mixing across output types (DAG vs PAG)** | False comparability | Locked U5: DAG-F1 is primary on projected DAG; PAG-native metrics are secondary and explicitly separated. |\n| **Multiple comparisons across cells x baselines** | Inflated false-positive rate | Holm correction across CAMUV-vs-baseline contrasts within family (section 8.2). |\n| **Single-seed fragility** | High variance for small `n` | >= 100 seeds per headline cell; bootstrap CIs; pre-registered decision rules (section 8.4). |\n| **Latent label non-identifiability at K_true >= 2** | ARI mis-reports performance | Best-permutation ARI; rotation-invariant metrics (e.g., subspace distance) reported alongside. |\n| **Wall-clock cliff on oversize cells** | Dropped cells | <= 4 h wall-clock per cell cap; cells exceeding it are demoted to a \"skipped\" status in the regime map rather than silently truncated. |\n\n---\n\n## 12. References / literature anchors\n\n**Policy.** Per the locked directive, CAMUV is *not* cited as a specific paper, version, or reference implementation. The following anchors are well-known causal-discovery algorithm **classes** referenced only to disambiguate terms used in the design; specific papers should be added by the reader if and when a reference implementation is chosen.\n\n- **PC algorithm** -- constraint-based causal discovery over observed variables (Spirtes-Glymour-Scheines line of work; widely catalogued in modern causal-discovery textbooks).\n- **GES** -- Greedy Equivalence Search, score-based equivalence-class search (Chickering line of work).\n- **FCI** -- Fast Causal Inference, constraint-based discovery that returns a PAG and is intended to handle latent common causes (Spirtes line of work; Zhang's later refinement is widely cited for soundness/completeness refinement).\n- **Linear-Gaussian factor models and PCA initialization** -- standard textbook material; no specific anchor cited.\n- **ARI / NMI** -- Hubert & Arabie (1985) for ARI; standard information-theoretic definitions for NMI.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A. Illustrative-only real-data sketch (optional, clearly labeled)\n\nIf, at the researcher's discretion, a real-world observational illustration is desirable without external assets, the very minimum requirement is a dataset that ships *with a Python package already installed or is constructible from built-in sources*. No such dataset is mandated; providing one would require explicit researcher direction. **No dataset is included in this design**, consistent with locked U6. If later added, it must be reported in a separate appendix section labeled `ILLUSTRATIVE -- REAL-DATA APPENDIX (NOT EXECUTED)`.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix B. Headline deliverable checklist\n\nThe design produces (without claiming execution):\n\n1. The markdown document you are reading, at `/workspace/experiment_design.md`.\n2. A pre-registered decision rule (section 8.4).\n3. A locked assumption manifest (section 10.1).\n4. A feasibility proof (section 9) showing the design fits inside locked budgets.\n5. An out-of-scope manifest (section 10.2) honest about what is *not* being claimed.\n6. **No** executed experiment; **no** claimed performance numbers.\nEOF4\necho '--- document complete ---' && wc -l /workspace/experiment_design.md && wc -c /workspace/experiment_design.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-76fc3e2d-294a-4259-a209-709b200f1daf", "tool_call": {"id": "call-76fc3e2d-294a-4259-a209-709b200f1daf", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Append design doc part 4 (sections 10-12 + appendices)\", \"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/experiment_design.md << 'EOF4'\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions & limitations (locked)\\n\\n### 10.1 Locked assumptions (A1-A6)\\n- **A1.** CAMUV is defined abstractly as a latent-aware causal-discovery procedure; **CAMUV-A** (augmented-variable search) and **CAMUV-B** (factor-residualization loop) are two concrete instantiations documented side-by-side throughout the design.\\n- **A2.** Evaluation target is synthetic; the regime map is the answer.\\n- **A3.** `K_max <= 3`; headline regime map covers `K_true in {0, 1, 2}`; extension to `K_true >= 3` is out-of-scope for the default battery (cell E1 optional, with explicit identifiability caveat).\\n- **A4.** Reported graph is the projected DAG on observed variables; latent structure is a secondary side-product.\\n- **A5.** No preinstalled scientific libraries; execution would require installation or from-scratch re-implementation, the latter adding a confounder that will be flagged.\\n- **A6.** Design-only deliverable; no executed experiment is claimed.\\n\\n### 10.2 Out-of-scope items (locked U2, U3, U4)\\n- Discrete-only or mixed-type data (continuous