{"id": "31d2ecd5-314c-45e5-9dfa-777396e5fbb4", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T18:09:20.584372", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "ae5e4a73-6f11-4f71-8d92-2a0ef399d918", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bcfe91a5-e444-41fd-ad71-0ab8085a0004", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bcfe91a5-e444-41fd-ad71-0ab8085a0004", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Re-handoff final CAMUV experiment design\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"## Final experiment design: CAMUV with unobserved common causes\\n\\n**Deliverable file.** `/workspace/experiment_design.md` (418 lines, 23,792 bytes). The full design with all 12 required sections is on disk. A summary of its content follows; the on-disk version is authoritative.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Section 1 — Objective and answer-shape\\nThe question is reframed as producing a **regime map**: a structured statement \\\"CAMUV vs baselines recovers the projected observational DAG with delta-F1 in [a, b] over the regime (n, K_true, function), and its advantage concentrates where K_true >= 1, n is past a phase transition, and each latent parent reaches enough observed children.\\\" The regime map is the headline figure. Secondary outputs: latent cardinality error |K_hat - K_true|, latent-membership ARI/NMI, negative-control null result.\\n\\n### Section 2 — Reference definition of CAMUV\\nTwo concrete instantiations of one abstract procedure, used consistently throughout the design with the labeled nomenclature CAMUV-A and CAMUV-B.\\n\\nCAMUV-A is augmented-variable search: latent factors are treated as additional unmeasured nodes and a causal-discovery routine runs on the augmented set with the constraint that latents are never directly conditioned on. K and Z are jointly refined by a penalized score (e.g., BIC on a linear-Gaussian SEM, or a kernel-CI score for nonlinear-additive).\\n\\nCAMUV-B is the factor-residualization loop: alternately fit a factor model X = Lambda Z + epsilon, run an observed-only CD routine on residuals, then update Lambda and Z from residual covariances until convergence.\\n\\nShared inputs (table): observational matrix D in R to the n x p, K_max (default 3), inner subroutine (PC-Fisher-z or GES-BIC-Gauss for Scheme A; factor-model class plus observed-only CD for Scheme B), alpha in {0.01, 0.05}, init in {PCA, random normal, k-means on rows of D}, tolerance 1e-4, max_iter 50.\\n\\nShared outputs: G_hat (DAG on observed variables), K_hat (estimated latent count), M_hat (per-observed-variable latent-membership vector).\\n\\nPseudocode skeleton provided inline with both branches. Inner subroutines init_latents, factor_model, refine_Z_from_augmented, update_factors, penalized_fit are intentionally left parametric to avoid asserting a single paper or version.\\n\\nHyperparameter grid (table): scheme in {A, B}, inner in {PC-Fisher-z, GES-BIC-Gauss}, init in {PCA, random normal, k-means on rows of D}, K_max in {1, 2, 3}, alpha in {0.01, 0.05}, tol=1e-4, max_iter=50. Default operating point chosen by 5-fold CV on a 2-cell held-out DGP subset and frozen before the headline battery.\\n\\n### Section 3 — DGP specification\\nEach cell generates a DAG on p_obs + K_true nodes (latents present only in the ground truth) and samples from\\n\\nX_j = f_j( Pa_obs(X_j) U Pa_lat(X_j) ) + epsilon_j ,   j = 1, ..., p\\n\\nPer-cell settings: edge weights i.i.d. from Uniform([-1.5, -0.3] U [0.3, 1.5]); functional class in {linear-Gaussian, nonlinear-additive with phi in {tanh, sin, polynomial degree <= 3}}; noise in {homoscedastic sigma=1, heteroscedastic sigma in Uniform(0.5, 2.0), heavy-tailed Student-t(nu=3) rescaled to unit variance}; sparsity in {sparse expected degree 1.5, dense expected degree 3}. Latent leakage rule: every latent has >= 3 observed children or the cell is flagged. Ground truth recorded per cell per seed: G_true (projected adjacency), O_true (orientation on observable triples), K_true, L_true (per-variable latent-membership matrix), parent weights, sigma_j. Out-of-scope variants: discrete or mixed-type, post-nonlinear functions, time series, cyclic or equilibrium SCMs, measurement error.\\n\\n### Section 4 — Factorial battery table\\nHeadline regime map uses a focused 3 x 3 x 2 sweep over (n, K_true, function) at canonical (p=10, sparsity=sparse, noise=homoscedastic, K_max=3) with 18 cells and >= 100 seeds each. Cells H1 through H9 cover n in {200, 1k, 10k} x K_true in {0, 1, 2} at linear-Gaussian; cells H10 through H18 mirror that grid at nonlinear-additive.\\n\\nSecondary battery varies p in {{5, 10}, 20}, the remaining noise regimes, and the dense sparsity, at a single (K_true=1, n=1000, linear-Gaussian) reference point (6 cells x 5 algorithms x 50 seeds).\\n\\nOptional flagged extension cell E1 (K_true = 3, linear-Gaussian) is included only if there is computational slack, with an explicit identifiability caveat that linear-Gaussian factor models with K >= 2 are identifiable only up to rotation, so Z is initialized with PCA and labels are calibrated to the data-implied subspace.\\n\\n### Section 5 — Baselines and oracles\\nPC (constraint-based, observed-only) and GES (score-based, observed-only) are the latent-naive baselines, expected to mis-orient nearest to colliders caused by unobserved common causes. FCI (constraint-based, latent-aware via PAG) is the latent-aware baseline; per locked U5, its PAG output is converted to a projected DAG by majority PAG-mark rule for primary comparison, with conversion loss reported separately, and PAG-native F1 reported as a secondary metric that is not intermingled with primary DAG F1. The fourth baseline slot is a well-known latent-aware reference method (LV-PCMCI), named only by algorithm class with no fabricated citation. Oracles: random-graph baseline (floor), fully-connected baseline (ceiling), and oracle-PC-with-truth-latents (ceiling under perfect latent observability). No re-implemented deep latent causal-discovery method is included, to preserve symmetry with the abstract CAMUV treatment.\\n\\n### Section 6 — Metrics\\nPrimary on the projected observational DAG (locked U5): skeleton F1, precision, recall, orientation accuracy on unambiguous triples, and normalized SHD. Secondary: latent-cardinality error |K_hat - K_true|, latent-membership ARI/NMI computed modulo best-permutation matching (for K_true >= 1), PAG-native F1 for FCI only reported in a separate channel. Statistical calibration: mean and 95% BCa bootstrap CIs over >= 100 seeds per cell, with paired-delta CIs for CAMUV vs baseline contrasts.\\n\\n### Section 7 — Controls\\nNegative control: colum-permute D to break edges, expecting all methods to drop to floor; deviation indicates leakage. Identifiability controls: latent leakage minimum (>= 3 observed children per latent), K_max >= K_true guard with non-compliant cells reported separately, PCA-initialized Z with best-permutation ARI under linear-Gaussian K_true >= 2. Sample-size sweep: the headline battery itself spans n in {200, 1000, 10000} to expose the phase transition in n at which CAMUV gains emerge and saturate. Runtime control: <= 4 h wall-clock per cell, <= 200 single-core-hours total (locked U7), 24-way parallelism across seeds. Hyperparameter-selection control: operating point frozen on a held-out mini-battery before the headline run, so per-cell timing does not contaminate per-cell tuning.\\n\\n### Section 8 — Analysis plan\\nMixed-effects model on per-seed metrics: metric_ijk = beta_0 + beta_method[m] + beta_K[K_true] + beta_n[n] + beta_func[function] + (beta_method x beta_K)[m, K] + (beta_method x beta_func)[m, function] + u_cell + epsilon_ijk, fit with REML (or its Python / NumPy equivalent if execution occurs), cluster-robust SE at the cell level. Multiple-comparison correction: Holm across baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-A and separately across baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-B; FWER controlled at alpha=0.05. Effect-size reporting: Cohen's d and 95% CI for the CAMUV vs best-baseline contrast per cell; headline deliverable is a Delta F1 heatmap over (n, K_true, function) paneled by function class. Pre-registered decision rule: Success if CAMUV-A or CAMUV-B exceeds the best non-oracle baseline by Delta F1 >= 0.05 with 95% CI excluding zero in >= 50% of headline cells with K_true >= 1 and n >= 1000; Mixed if the above holds for one of K_true in {1, 2} but not the other; Negative if neither scheme meets the threshold, with the regime map reported honestly and limitations foregrounded.\\n\\n### Section 9 — Feasibility and resource estimate\\nIllustrative per-fit timing model is provided as a code sketch (prefixed ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH -- NOT EXECUTED). Estimated total: ~10 single-core-hours for headline battery (~1.7), secondary battery (~2.6), negative controls (~0.3), held-out hyperparameter CV (~1.0), overhead and re-runs (~4.4). This fits well under the 200 single-core-hour cap and under the 4-hour-per-cell wall-clock cap when seeds are parallelized at up to 24 concurrent workers. The bubblewrap / no-network constraint is flagged: any later execution must be fully self-contained pipelines. Software prerequisites (numpy, scipy, networkx, scikit-learn, pandas, plus per-method implementations) are documented but not installed; the re-implementation-versus-published-baseline confounder is acknowledged.\\n\\n### Section 10 — Locked assumptions and out-of-scope items\\nLocked A1 through A6 are restated verbatim. Out-of-scope: discrete or mixed-type data (continuous only); K_true >= 3 by default (only flagged extension cell E1); post-nonlinear functional class (only linear-Gaussian and nonlinear-additive); time series, cyclic SCMs, measurement error. The CAMUV-A/B parameterization is explicitly a working definition, not a claim about any specific published algorithm.\\n\\n### Section 11 — Threats to validity and mitigations\\nNine rows covering algorithmic-reimplementation confounder, hyperparameter over-tuning, DGP-family bias, identifiability violations, metric comparability across DAG vs PAG outputs (locked U5), multiple comparisons, single-seed fragility on small n, latent-label non-identifiability at K_true >= 2, and wall-clock cliffs on oversize cells. Each row pairs a directional bias argument with a concrete mitigation. CAMUV-A versus CAMUV-B conflation is addressed by reporting both side-by-side throughout the design and analyzing them as separate fixed-effect levels in the mixed model; the convergence-rate tertiary metric distinguishes scheme-specific failure modes.\\n\\n### Section 12 — References / literature anchors\\nRestricted to well-known algorithm classes: PC, GES, FCI, LV-PCMCI, linear-Gaussian factor models and PCA initialization, ARI/NMI (Hubert and Arabie 1985 mentioned). No CAMUV citation fabricated.\\n\\n### Appendices\\nAppendix A is an optional real-data illustration placeholder, explicitly marked optional and not included, consistent with locked U6. Appendix B is the headline deliverable checklist, restating that no experiment is claimed to have been executed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Verification commands run on the deliverable\\nls -la /workspace/experiment_design.md returned 23,792 bytes. wc -l returned 418. grep -c '^## ' returned 14 (the 12 numbered sections plus Appendix A and Appendix B). grep -nE '^## ' confirmed header sequence: 1 Objective restatement, 2 Reference definition of CAMUV, 3 DGP specification, 4 Factorial battery table, 5 Baselines and oracles, 6 Metrics, 7 Controls, 8 Analysis plan, 9 Feasibility, 10 Assumptions and limitations (locked), 11 Threats to validity, 12 References, A illustrative real-data placeholder, B deliverable checklist. grep -c 'ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH' returned 4 (three code blocks prefixed plus one mention in the in-document note stating that all Python blocks carry the prefix).\\n\\n### Honoring locked resolutions\\nU1 / A1 — both schemes documented with consistent CAMUV-A and CAMUV-B labeling; no paper, version, or implementation asserted. A3 — K_max <= 3; K_true = 3 only in optional flagged E1. U2 — continuous-only default. U3 — K_true in {0, 1, 2}. U4 — linear-Gaussian plus nonlinear-additive only. U5 — primary DAG-F1 isolated; FCI PAG on a separate secondary channel. U6 — synthetic-only; regime map is the answer; Appendix A is empty placeholder. U7 — <= 200 single-core-hour cap respected with substantial slack; <= 4 h wall-clock per cell respected under 24-way parallelism; >= 100 seeds per headline cell. U8 — single markdown at /workspace/experiment_design.md; every Python block prefixed ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH -- NOT EXECUTED.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository state confirmed via ls -la /workspace and find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f: only task.json present. Decoded task.json confirmed inline documents.track.md text 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.' Environment probe: python3==3.12.3 at /usr/bin/python3; explicit imports of numpy, scipy, networkx, pandas, scikit-learn, pgmpy, causalnex, castle, matplotlib each raised ModuleNotFoundError. public_task.environment confirms network disabled, GPU disabled, 24 CPU cores, no datasets, bubblewrap runtime. Document at /workspace/experiment_design.md was written in four sequential cat append chunks (each under the 12000-character shell limit). Final integrity verification: ls -la reports 23792 bytes; wc -l reports 418 lines; grep -c '^## ' reports 14 (twelve numbered sections plus Appendix A and Appendix B); grep -nE '^## ' confirmed the required header order from Section 1 to Section 12 followed by the two appendices; grep -c 'ILLUSTRATIVE SKETCH' reports 4 (three prefixed Python code blocks plus one prose mention of the prefix in the in-document note). No experimental execution was attempted; no scientific library was installed; no system call beyond document write and read-only inspection commands was issued.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"A6 - Design only deliverable; n