pha in {0.01, 0.05}. Operating point chosen by 5-fold CV on a held-out 2-cell mini-battery and frozen before headline runs.\\n\\nSection 3. DGP specification. Each cell generates a DAG on p_obs + K_true nodes (latents present only in ground truth) and samples from X_j = f_j(Pa_obs(X_j) union Pa_lat(X_j)) + epsilon_j. Edge weights i.i.d. from Uniform([-1.5, -0.3] union [0.3, 1.5]). Functional class: linear-Gaussian or nonlinear-additive (phi in {tanh, sin, polynomial degree <= 3}). Noise: homoscedastic sigma=1, heteroscedastic sigma in Uniform(0.5, 2.0), or heavy-tailed Student-t(nu=3) rescaled to unit variance. Sparsity: sparse (expected degree ~1.5) or dense (expected degree ~3). Latent-leakage rule: each latent has >= 3 observed children. Ground truth recorded per cell per seed: G_true, O_true, K_true, L_true, parent weights, sigma_j. Out-of-scope DGP variants: discrete or mixed-type data, post-nonlinear, time series, cyclic or equilibrium SCMs, measurement error.\\n\\nSection 4. Factorial battery table. Headline regime map is an 18-cell 3 x 3 x 2 sweep over (n in {200, 1k, 10k}, K_true in {0, 1, 2}, function in {linear-Gaussian, nonlinear-additive}) at canonical (p=10, sparsity=sparse, noise=homoscedastic, K_max=3) with at least 100 seeds per cell. Secondary battery varies p in {{5, 10}, 20}, the remaining noise regimes, and dense sparsity at a single reference point (K_true=1, n=1000, linear-Gaussian), totaling 6 cells x 5 algorithms x 50 seeds. Optional flagged extension cell E1 at K_true=3 linear-Gaussian if there is computational slack, with explicit identifiability caveat about linear-Gaussian factor-model rotations.\\n\\nSection 5. Baselines and oracles. PC (observed-only constraint-based) and GES (observed-only score-based) are latent-naive. FCI is the latent-aware baseline via PAG; per locked U5 the PAG is converted to a projected DAG by majority PAG-mark rule for primary comparison, conversion loss reported separately, PAG-native F1 reported on a separate secondary channel. LV-PCMCI is named only by algorithm class as the latent-aware comparator, with no fabricated citation. Oracles: random-graph floor, fully-connected ceiling, oracle-PC-with-truth-latents ceiling. No re-implemented deep latent causal-discovery method is included, to preserve symmetry with the abstract CAMUV treatment.\\n\\nSection 6. Metrics. Primary on the projected observational DAG: skeleton F1, precision, recall, orientation accuracy on unambiguous triples, and normalized SHD. Secondary: latent-cardinality error |K_hat - K_true|, latent-membership ARI/NMI modulo best-permutation matching (for K_true >= 1), PAG-native F1 for FCI on a separate channel. Statistical calibration: mean and 95% BCa bootstrap CIs over >= 100 seeds per cell, paired-delta CIs for CAMUV-vs-baseline contrasts.\\n\\nSection 7. Controls. Negative control: column-permute D to break edges, expecting all methods to drop to floor. Identifiability controls: latent-leakage minimum, K_max >= K_true guard with non-compliant cells reported separately, PCA-initialized Z with best-permutation ARI under linear-Gaussian K_true >= 2. Sample-size sweep: headline battery spans n in {200, 1000, 10000}. Runtime control: <= 4 h wall-clock per cell and <= 200 single-core-hours total, with 24-way parallelism across seeds. Hyperparameter-selection control: operating point frozen on a held-out mini-battery before the headline run.\\n\\nSection 8. Analysis plan. Mixed-effects model on per-seed metrics: metric_ijk = beta_0 + beta_method[m] + beta_K[K_true] + beta_n[n] + beta_func[function] + (beta_method x beta_K)[m, K] + (beta_method x beta_func)[m, function] + u_cell + epsilon_ijk, fit with REML or its Python / NumPy equivalent, with cluster-robust SE at the cell level. Multiple-comparison correction is Holm across baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-A and separately across baselines used as contrast for CAMUV-B, FWER alpha=0.05. Effect-size reporting: Cohen's d and 95% CI for the CAMUV-vs-best-